// The app plus the canned backend: what `CountryFlagsAppMock` builds.
//
// The mock backend is a Swift package product, and Xcode links every product a
// target depends on whatever the configuration it is building — so the only
// way to keep the fixtures out of the store build is to keep the dependency
// out of the target that ships. That is the whole reason there are two app
// targets; see Config/README.md.
//
// Everything below is deliberately independent of the configuration this
// target is built with. A mock app built as "Prod" would still be the mock
// app, and pinning the environment and the name here means it can never be
// mistaken for the one that ships: it is called "CountryFlags Mock", it
// installs under its own bundle identifier, and `check-release-app.sh` looks
// for `CountryFlags.app`, which this target does not produce.
#include "Mock.xcconfig"
#include "App.xcconfig"

// The flag the app target reads to decide whether the mock backend exists.
// It is a property of this target, not of the Mock configuration: the
// shipping target has to compile under Mock too, and it has no mock module to
// import.
SWIFT_ACTIVE_COMPILATION_CONDITIONS = $(inherited) MOCK_BACKEND
